WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.560
50 years after we last left footprints on the moon, NASA's Artemis-1 is our first bold

00:09.560 --> 00:15.560
step towards getting us back there and pushing us farther than we've ever been before.

00:15.560 --> 00:20.000
You are looking at the world's most powerful rocket, an Orion spacecraft, live on Launch

00:20.000 --> 00:22.560
Pad 39B.

00:22.560 --> 00:27.000
The energy here is palpable as we attempt to make history today.

00:28.000 --> 00:33.000
We're going to learn so much about the solar system from the moon and even about the earth.

00:33.000 --> 00:36.000
Range weather, weather is go for launch.

00:36.000 --> 00:38.000
The mission management team has been pulled.

00:38.000 --> 00:40.000
We have a go to proceed with terminal count.

00:40.000 --> 00:45.000
On behalf of all the men and women across our great nation who have worked to bring this hardware

00:45.000 --> 00:53.000
together to make this day possible, at this time I give you a go to resume count and launch Artemis-1.

00:53.000 --> 00:55.000
Four stage engines start.

00:55.000 --> 00:59.000
Three, two, one, boosters ignition.

00:59.000 --> 01:01.000
And lift off of Artemis-1.

01:01.000 --> 01:06.000
We rise together, back to the moon and beyond.

01:10.000 --> 01:14.000
NASA is now traveling 607 miles per hour.

01:16.000 --> 01:19.000
The harder the climb, the better the view.

01:19.000 --> 01:21.000
We showed the space coast tonight.

01:21.000 --> 01:24.000
What a beautiful view it is.

01:24.000 --> 01:30.000
The first step in returning our country to the moon and on to Mars.

01:36.000 --> 01:45.000
We are understanding now from the James Webb Space Telescope just how big and vast this universe is.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.000
There's a lot out there to explore.

01:48.000 --> 01:52.000
And this is the next beginning.

01:54.000 --> 02:00.000
Orion is now flying free, attached to the European Service Module and on its journey to the moon.

02:18.000 --> 02:24.000
NASA

